  "summary": "Cold review (iteration 2) of the expansive study for liturgy/roman-rite/1962/propers/temporal/01-first-sunday-of-advent (provider gemini; main.tex, proper-components.toml, publication.toml, sections/01-09, and reviewed research records) passes with zero findings. Appointed-text completeness ('complete' / Full PDF) matches the 2026-11-25 production-plan authorization and reviewed research; all ten appointed Latin propers, the governed Preface of the Most Holy Trinity, and public-domain English study texts (Douay-Rheims 1899 American edition controlled against Gutenberg 1581; Lasance/Walsh 1945) match tracked witnesses verbatim. Both standing blocking findings from iteration 1 (STU-008 in research/context.md, research/scope.md, research/interpretations.md, and propers/verified.md; STU-009 across sections/01-04) and prior advisory STU-010 (sections/06-lane-conversion.tex:22 and research/interpretations.md:125) are fully resolved and verified directly against Clementine Vulgate Ps 24:1-4 and Ps 47:10, Douay-Rheims 1899, WEB Catholic Ps 25 and Ps 48, and St. Thomas Aquinas, Postilla super Psalmos on Ps 24:4 (Parma vol. 14, p. 230, aquinas-parma14.txt), with zero stale '24:1--3a', 'v. 3a', or 'Heb. 48:9' citations remaining. Consequential-difficulty tests passed against primary witnesses at exact loci: (1) Lane 1 (eschatological-detachment) checked against St. Gregory the Great, Homiliae in Evangelia Hom. 1.1-6 (Wikisource Latin 1.1-5-luke-21.toml, Bute 1908, Oxford 1843), St. Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Luke Sermon CXXXIX (Payne Smith pp. 653-654), St. Bede, In Lucae Evangelium Expositio VI (luke-21-28/29/32/33.toml), and St. John Chrysostom, Homilies on Romans Hom. 24 (24.rom-13.11-14.toml) \u2014 the prose teaches why cosmic collapse turns into joy (levate capita = exhilarate corda, fructus mundi ruina est), balances Gregory with Cyril's bodily resurrection and Bede's renewal per imaginem, and explains Chrysostom's opera tenebrarum vs. arma lucis contrast; (2) Lane 2 (incarnational-marian) checked against St. Ambrose, Expositio Evangelii secundum Lucam X.37-45 (book-10-html-083e1630) and St. Robert Bellarmine, Explanatio in Psalmos on Ps 84:8, 13 (psalm-84-verse-8/13.toml) \u2014 the prose clearly develops Ambrose's Marian/prophetic nube (Isa 19:1; Luke 1:35) and Luke's unique et omnes arbores double figure alongside Bellarmine's Marian terra nostra; (3) Lane 3 (interior-conversion) checked against St. Augustine, Enarrationes in Psalmos 84.9, 13-16 (1888-oxford-coxe/passages/psalm-84-9..16.toml), St. Thomas Aquinas, Postilla super Psalmos Ps 24 (Parma vol. 14, pp. 229-230), and Bl. Ildefonso Schuster, The Sacramentary I (pp. 321-323) \u2014 the prose discloses Augustine's Old Latin suavitatem, quotes Coxe 1888 accurately with bracketed ellipsis, accurately maps Aquinas's intellect/affections petition under Vias tuas before Et semitas (counsels), and distinguishes Schuster's Eucharistic exposition from St. Bernard's Middle Coming. All three interpretations are distinct, coherent, supported by registered carrying authors (authority-standing-v1), carry their own four senses, observe strict 1962 isolation, preserve Amalarius's 838 Kiersy (Quierzy) censure wherever cited, confine differing medieval Advent I Gospels to one clause of the terminal scope appendix, and read cleanly under guidance/editorial.md. Read-only validation suites (document-types refresh --check, check-proper-components, check-content-preflight [all 12 checks], check-generation-metadata, check-web-edition, and scripts/_proper_study.py check) all passed. Meaningful limits preserved transparently in scope and source records: CMAA 1962 facsimile remains remote/unresolved visual control only; RG 128 Latin text, Murphy 1914 Douay-Rheims, restricted 2026 FSSP/ICRSP Ordo HTML payloads, and remote patristic Psalm/Romans editions were not inspected offline; physical print pagination is outside source-mode review."
